# Exercise 01 — Limit Order Book (price-time priority)

**Lesson:** [0016 — Order-book dynamics](../../docs/learning/lessons/0016-order-book-dynamics.html)
**Difficulty:** foundational — do this one first; exercises 02+ build on its concepts.

## Goal

Implement `OrderBook` in `order_book.py`: a limit order book where price wins
first and, at the same price, time (arrival order) wins — exactly the matching
rule used by most equity exchanges.

## Spec

- `add_limit(order_id, side, price, qty)` — rest a limit order. `side` is
  `"buy"` or `"sell"`. Reject (ValueError) bad sides, non-positive qty/price,
  duplicate ids.
- `cancel(order_id)` — remove a resting order; `KeyError` if unknown. An
  emptied price level must vanish so the BBO re-derives.
- `best_bid()` / `best_ask()` / `bbo()` — top of book; `None` on an empty side.
- `level_qty(side, price)` — total resting qty at one level.
- `execute_market(side, qty)` — aggressor order: a buy lifts asks cheapest
  first, a sell hits bids highest first; FIFO within a level; partial fills
  leave the remainder at the **front** of the queue; returns `list[Fill]`.
- `imbalance(levels=1)` — `(bid_qty − ask_qty) / (bid_qty + ask_qty)` over the
  top N levels per side; `0.0` on an empty book; always in `[−1, 1]`.

